00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  monday june 27th i'm julie chang for the  wall street journal filling in for zoe  thomas  virtual reality is popular for games and  fitness it's also becoming more popular  in senior communities rendevor is one vr  company whose platform is increasingly  being used to help seniors who might be  struggling rendezvous co-founder and ceo  kyle rand says some have used the  company's vr platform to travel back to  childhood streets connect with loved  ones who are physically far away and  more and he says that's helped improve  their mental health  at the wall street journal's recent  future of everything festival rand spoke  with wsj personal tech columnist nicole  nguyen about how virtual reality can and  is being used to combat loneliness in  senior living communities here are  highlights from their conversation so  when people who are skeptical of virtual  reality think of vr i think they think  of a wally style future where you're  floating around holding your giant soda  with a screen strapped to your face  completely ignorant of everything that's  happening around you but your company is  working on a very different take than  that it's a virtual reality enabled  therapy and wellness platform i believe  um can you tell us a little bit more  about what that therapy entails and and  the role vr has in it yeah it's such an  interesting  perspective a lot of people so we're  about to turn six years old our company  around ever  so six years ago vr was still very new  and we came up with this idea of using  it for older adults to empower a better  lifestyle for them a lot of people  thought oh this is just escapism right  the ability to put on a headset and  just be anywhere else other than a  senior living community but really what  we're doing is we've designed this  platform that enables people to put  these headsets on and they can go  anywhere in the world they can stand on  top of the eiffel tower they can go on a  hot air balloon ride they can go back to  their childhood home and stay in front  of their childhood home again for the  first time  and  they do it together so really it's a  social experience platform where people  are able to spend time doing real world  things exploring the world and then  building relationships as a result of it  how are you structuring these platforms  um what if you don't want to visit your  childhood home for example uh how do you  know what content to serve interestingly  enough we've done demos for the past six  years like every single age group if i  tell someone they can go anywhere in the  world nine times out of ten they're  gonna say bring me home  especially if they're very far away from  home and so it's a really meaningful  meaningful location but our approach to  content creation and experience delivery  is if they're like  when you think about the aging process  what happens day in and day out is your  world starts to shrink right and what we  can do in vr is we can open up those  doors and  the diversity of the aging demographic  is so wide that somebody might want to  go to paris somebody might want to go to  israel somebody might want to spend like  my grandfather when i first did a  session with him we went on a hot air  balloon ride we went swimming with  dolphins we  we went on a roller coaster ride and  then i brought him back to his home in  long island he still lives in long  island so it was like i don't know 20  miles away and then we spent two hours  we were up until two in the morning  exploring the streets of the areas that  he grew up in and like that's what he  leaned into because that's what's so  significant for him  and for this demographic  when you create a connection to the  place that you're visiting the  experience that you're having it opens  up the floor for communication that's  really positive that oftentimes can be  really hard to find before the session  we were talking about um your new puppy  your new dog dad but also that you have  a puppy agenda um as it relates to  content that's on this platform yes so  one of the very first things that we  launched was a room full of golden  retriever puppies and golden lab puppies  and i actually have an amazing story so  that same community that first started  uh we were doing this demonstration  before we started working with them and  we had like this whole group of  residents everybody was experiencing vr  they're having a great time and about  halfway in they wheeled in a woman and  her name was mickey and her caregiver  pulled me aside i was like i need you to  understand her story and  he was like this woman was one of the  first people to move into our community  she's been with us since day one and she  always has had a smile on her face like  she knows everyone by name she's the  first person to say hi to you in the  morning she's just like this beacon of  light in our community and then two  months  prior to that she had been diagnosed  with early stage dementia and she was  moved upstairs into the locked memory  care floor  and what happened was she had her first  symptom was expressive aphasia so she  actually lost her ability to communicate  so she went for me this outgoing  boisterous woman to all of a sudden not  being able  to talk and so what we did was we  brought her into that room full of  puppies  and it was like this like a light switch  turned right back on this huge smile  comes across her face she like started  calling out to the puppies like kissing  at them and like virtual rooms  a virtual room full of puppies very  important and just got so engaged and so  excited and she actually was talking to  the dogs and the most amazing thing  happened at the end of the session she  took that headset off and she kept  talking  and she actually followed me and my  co-founder out of the building holding  onto my co-founder's arm and she was  talking about how we needed to use this  technology for children and to use it  within school systems because it would  be amazing to learn through vr if you  think about that what happened was after  just a 30 minute session in vr she went  from three months of not talking at all  to pitching us business ideas so content  aside there's a lot that needs to be  improved in the hardware itself you use  a third party virtual reality headset  correct correct  things like battery life and the weight  of the headset  and  especially when it comes to  accessibility i have a left eye that's  much worse than my right eye so when i  use vr i feel nauseous i can't wear it  for a long time and i imagine that a lot  of your audience is visually impaired or  has some other sort of a similar need  how are you working around that yeah  it's interesting i think we're really  just starting to learn about the  implications for visual impairments i  think if you if it's laterally different  that's that's something we haven't seen  or tested yet but one of the most  interesting things we've seen is people  with glaucoma and with macular  degeneration  vr is actually one of their favorite  things and  the the research isn't there yet you  know it's easy to guess that the reason  they're able to actually see in vr is  because the screen is so close and so  their fovea like really widens but i  think we're really as we look to the  future you know the hardware has really  done amazing things in the past couple  of years there's a lot of places to go  there's a lot of research to be done to  understand how it works with a variety  of different conditions if the audience  has questions feel free to raise their  hands  and and ask  is the content is it cg based or video  based so it's a combination honestly  it's a combination of video based  experiences honestly video or sorry  photo based experiences like 360 photos  and fully rendered  application environments and  interestingly a lot of people think that  360 videos are where most people would  lean in but we have we have analytics  that show exactly what people are doing  and we see that the highest adoption the  largest amount of time spent in vr is  actually on 360 imagery where people are  able to be there and just spend a lot of  time there and i think you know there's  a lot that we could talk about there but  i think one of the big reasons is that  if you're in a 360 video experience you  are limited to the constraints of what  is in that video versus if you're in a  still environment you're going to spend  a lot of time just exploring the  environment looking and talking about  the things that are happening and it  creates so much opportunity for a  conversation to happen in those  experiences thank you so much for  joining us thank you nicole thank you  all thank you everybody  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want to listen to the  full talk check out the link in our show  notes and as always for more tech  stories you can go to our website  wsj.com  i'm julie chang for the wall street  journal thanks for listening  [Music]  you

